latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the Input Grid:\n   a. Scan the grid to identify all unique non-background colors.\n   b. For each color, find all connected regions using a flood fill algorithm.\n   c. Select the largest connected region for each color.\n\n2. Determine Color Order:\n   a. For each color's largest region, calculate:\n      - Distance from the edge of the grid\n      - Size of the region\n      - Topmost and leftmost coordinates\n   b. Sort colors based on these criteria:\n      - Primary: Minimum distance from edge (ascending)\n      - Secondary: Size of region (descending)\n      - Tertiary: Topmost coordinate (ascending)\n      - Quaternary: Leftmost coordinate (ascending)\n\n3. Create Output Grid:\n   a. Calculate the size of the output grid: 2n - 1, where n is the number of unique colors.\n   b. Initialize an empty square grid of this size.\n\n4. Fill Output Grid:\n   a. Iterate through the sorted colors from outermost to innermost.\n   b. For each color:\n      - Calculate the size of its frame: output_size - (2 * color_index)\n      - Draw the frame by filling the outermost empty cells of the current grid state\n\n5. Handle Innermost Color:\n   a. If there's only one color, fill the center cell.\n   b. If there are two colors, fill the center 2x2 square with the inner color.\n   c. If there are three or more colors:\n      - Fill a 2x2 square in the center if possible\n      - If not (grid size is even), fill just the center cell\n\n6. Validate Output:\n   a. Ensure all cells in the grid are filled.\n   b. Verify that each color forms a complete frame around inner colors.\n\n7. Return Result:\n   Return the completed ColoredGrid object.\n\nThis plan addresses the key points from our reflection:\n- It handles color selection and ordering based on position and size.\n- It creates a square output with nested frames.\n- It provides a more flexible approach to handling the innermost color.\n- It should work for any number of colors (up to 9, as per the problem constraints).\n- It doesn't rely on specific shape recognition, focusing instead on the largest connected region for each color.\n\nThis approach should be more robust and generalized, capable of handling a wider range of input scenarios while still solving the given test cases correctly."
